            <ArticleTitle>[Postoperative complications and unanticipated admission in ambulatory surgery].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1006-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Day-case surgery is becoming more popular in Japan. The incidence of postoperative complications and unanticipated hospital admission was evaluated between June 1, 1998 and May 31, 2001.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Two hundred fifty seven cases of ambulatory surgery were retrospectively evaluated in terms of methods of anesthesia, anesthesia time, recovery time, postoperative complications and unanticipated hospital admission.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">General anesthesia was used in 44% of 257 ambulatory surgical cases. Mean anesthesia time was 81 minutes and mean recovery time was 227 minutes. The most frequent complication was postoperative pain, incidence of which was 53%. The incidence of unanticipated hospital admission was 7.8%. Among this admission, anesthesia-related reasons were cited in 44%.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Effective pain management and well-chosen anesthetic technique are important for the success of ambulatory surgery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
